It would be nice if you could control how hard you hit a monster.
I don't know if how hard you hit has anything to do with whether
or not it leaves a corpse, but it sure seems like it.  Whenever I
get a studly character, he ends up starving because he dispatches
the animals bodies and all.  It seems reasonable that one could
"pull one's punches."  I wouldn't always want to do this; maybe the
normal keys would be normal hits, with a meta-key command asking
"what direction" and "how hard."

About training your pet: another nice command would be "scat" - I get
*really tired* when my pet is persistantly in the place I want to go.
Maybe this could be trained by hitting him weakly (if possible) then
saying the keyword.  Also: if "trainabilities" vary from species to 
species, will these change if you polymorph your pet?  If you 'morph
him back, will they come back, or are they lost?  I always considered
a polymorph to change the body, but not the mind; so a cat 'morphed
into a dog would lose the scratching claws, but a dog 'morphed into a
cat would still fetch.

Other nice things to train a dog to do would be to "give" and to "dig."

About digging: how about being able to dig a pit (you, with a pick-axe,
or your trained dog), put things in it, then pushing a boulder into it
to fill it up?  Such buried things would then be unavailable to most
monsters.  Such buried things would be like things in a chest.  If you
like, you could later dig them up again.  In fact, maybe leprechauns
could have buried gold around (there being no pirates in the game).  If
you capture a leprechaun (with a leash, or maybe it'll take a blessed
and/or enchanted leash to keep him from teleporting) he'll have to lead
you to it.

Perhaps we could have "doggie cookies" (like Milk-Bones (tm)) to give
out.  The wouldn't be so nutritious, but they would be lighter.

While I'm thinking of dogs, shouldn't some corpses leave bones, which 
your dog might like to chew on?  While you're at, some bones (like
jawbones..) might make so-so weapons.  Some skulls might have some
uses in magic.  Also, according to legend, if you boil a live black cat
until it's only skin and bones (and stew..), there is a bone that will
make you invisible when you put it in your mouth.  In the game, maybe
eating a boiled black cat skeleton could cause invisibility.  (to boil:
put him in a fountain, sink, or other water and zap with a wand of fire.)

It would be nice to modify the behaviour of the game when you try to hit
some animals.  Sometimes it asks you if you want to hit [yn].  If you are
in the habit of killing these monsters to, you could get rid of the question.
More importantly, it would be nice if it would ask you the question, 
instead of assuming the answer ("no") when you try to hit your pet.  
Sometimes I get so fed up with my pet I just want to kill him to get him
out of my way forever.  And: if you could *add* certain monsters to the
question, like floating eyes.  Many times, on a slow machine (either because
of disk accesses on PCs or loaded machines), I've hit the arrow key one too
many times, only to hit the `e' and be frozen.  Of course, then the world
(notably, David's Treasure Zoo) teleports in on top of me and hacks away.

Back a couple of years ago, when I added a bunch of stuff to Don Kneller's
PC Hack, one thing I added was the idea that wooden things, when wielded,
would scare a vampire.  The most powerful was the "wooden stake" weapon,
but also wooden wands or rings (whatever they happened to be) would be
useful.  There was a chance that you could stab the vampire through the
heart with a stake or wand, killing him but losing the weapon.  This 
would be neat here.  Similarly, a silver wand should scare a werewolf,
and a good hit with one should kill him.  Or maybe it would just kill
him until you removed the wand by unwittingly picking it up...  Also,
silver other (weapons, armor, rings) may be useful.  (And I believe that
mithril is an alloy of half-silver, half-something else..?)

I hope these suggestions are useful, and stir up discussion and development.

